// Probe: THE `pipeline values:` BLOCK FOR A KEYWORD PIPE STAGE.
//
// EVERY ASSERTION IN THIS FILE FAILS ON PURPOSE. The corpus runs only
// passing assertions, so it never shows a failure report; that is the same
// reason testdata/pipe_stages.nomi exists. What is checked here is the
// REPORT TEXT.
//
// pipestage.go admits `|> if` and `|> case` among the recordable shapes.
//
// The rule: the run records a pipeline stage for EVERY stage
// shape, so the stage list is a function of the spine here exactly as it is for
// a call chain. Both spellings are below because they produce different chains:
// the keyword form desugars to TWO pipe nodes and therefore THREE rows (the
// leftmost value, the condition prefix, the whole chain), and the bare form to
// ONE pipe node and therefore TWO. A fixture with only one spelling cannot see
// a lowering that recorded the wrong number of rows.
//
// The third case is the one that would be a WRONG report rather than an absent
// one: a chain mixing an ordinary call stage with a keyword stage, where the
// prefix texts must be the CUMULATIVE source of each prefix and not the stage's
// own.
//
// Note the rendered text of a keyword stage's last row is the KEYWORD spelling
// (`|> if Int.equal?(12) { False } else { True }`), not the desugared double
// pipe — the formatter renders the parser's two nodes back to the one the user
// wrote, through format.RenderNode.
//
// THE LAST TWO TESTS ARE A DIFFERENT CONTRACT and they are the pair that makes
// gen.pipedCase observable. A directly written `case` records its SCRUTINEE
// as a `values:` row, and a BARE pipe stage's node has a nil Value, so the
// piped spelling records no such row. A builder that spliced Value in
// without exempting the node would record a row the piped spelling must
// not have.
//
// The scrutinee is `wrap(3)` rather than `Some(3)` deliberately: the report
// drops a row whose operand text reads exactly like its value, so
// `Some(3) = Some(3)` would be suppressed in both spellings and they would
// present identically. `wrap(3) = Some(3)` is the row that distinguishes them.
fn wrap(n: Int): Maybe<Int> {
    Some(n)
}

test "a keyword-prefixed if stage reports three pipeline rows" {
    flag =
        12
        |> if Int.equal?(12) { False } else { True }

    assert flag
}

test "a bare case stage reports two pipeline rows" {
    flag =
        Some(3)
        |> case {
                Some(_) -> False
                None -> True
            }

    assert flag
}

test "a call stage before a keyword stage reports cumulative prefixes" {
    flag =
        [1, 2, 3]
        |> Iter.count()
        |> if Int.equal?(3) { False } else { True }

    assert flag
}

test "a piped case scrutinee contributes no values row" {
    assert (wrap(3) |> case {
            Some(n) -> n
            None -> 0
        }) == 99
}

test "a directly written case scrutinee does contribute one" {
    assert (case wrap(3) {
        Some(n) -> n
        None -> 0
    }) == 99
}
